{
  "dom_id": "question-336481",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from the definition of ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ as the standard fundamental matrix by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by substitution from lines {{field-3}}.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
